; desktop - the CatOS graphical shell. An ordinary userland program.
;
; It grows its own memory (sbrk) to obtain a full-screen framebuffer plus a
; scratch buffer, then points the display hardware straight at that framebuffer
; (gacquire) - so from then on the screen simply *is* this process's memory. The
; kernel does no drawing at all: every pixel below is pushed by plain stores and
; the `cpy` instruction, here in userland.
;
;
; -- Pages, not windows -------------------------------------------------------
;
; The screen is 512x384. That is far too small to float overlapping windows on,
; so the desktop is paged instead: one full-screen page at a time, with a tab bar
; along the bottom to move between them.
;
; A page is a surface, and nothing else. Any program that calls wcreate gets one;
; it commits pixels into its surface and we pull them out with wread and blit them
; into the content area. So "spawn a window" and "spawn a page" are the same act,
; and a client needs to know nothing about paging - gdemo is unchanged in that
; respect.
;
; The layout this manager imposes is the simplest one there is: every window gets
; the whole page (fit_pages). Only one page is on screen at a time, so a window has
; nothing to share the space with, and a small window centred on a 512x368 page just
; leaves two thirds of a small screen as unusable background - which is what the
; editor and the stopwatch used to do. The size passed to wcreate is a first guess;
; wresize is the answer, and the client reshapes itself through wconfig exactly as
; it would under tile.cat.
;
; That subsumes the maximum this always had to enforce: the tab bar makes the page
; shorter than the display the kernel bounds a surface by, and a full-height window
; is not merely clipped but invisible, since the scratch buffer wread copies into is
; exactly one page big.
;
; There used to be a page 0 that rasterised the console's text mirror (conread), so
; that the desktop carried a shell around with it. term.cat replaced it: a terminal
; is a window like any other, you can have several, and each one gets a pty of its
; own instead of every view sharing the machine's single console session. Keeping a
; built-in one would have meant this manager owning a special page that behaves
; unlike every other page - a tab that cannot be closed, a page with no program
; behind it, a focus rule with an exception in it - to do worse what an ordinary
; client now does well. So the console page is gone, and with nothing open the
; desktop is an empty background: CTRL+` and "term" is how you get a shell, exactly
; as it is under tile.
;
;
; -- Input --------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; CTRL+Left / CTRL+Right change page. Those two keycodes never reach the console:
; the keyboard ISR diverts them to the window manager while the screen is owned, and
; we drain them with gkey.
;
; Only the CTRL chord is ours, which is the whole reason the kernel hands over
; modifier bits rather than deciding for us. A bare Left is a cursor movement and
; SHIFT+Left is a selection, and both of those belong to the window on the page: an
; editor needs every arrow there is, and a manager that took the unmodified ones
; would leave it half a keyboard. That is the general rule a window manager's
; shortcuts have to follow - an unmodified key is text, and text is the window's -
; and it is why the launcher is a chord too.
;
; Every *other* key belongs to the page you are looking at: the whole keyboard is
; grabbed (keygrab -1) and each event is handed to that page's window with
; wkeypost, whole and uninterpreted, so a program with a window can be typed into.
; editor.cat and term.cat both need this. That grab is dropped whenever there is no
; window at all, so an empty desktop leaves the keys to the console line discipline
; and the boot shell behind it goes on working - the same rule tile.cat follows, and
; the reason it is a rule is that a manager holding the keyboard with nothing to
; give it to would be a machine you cannot type at.
;
; The grab follows the page, which is the entire focus policy: the window you are
; looking at is the window you are typing into.
;
; CTRL+` opens the launcher over whatever page is up: type to filter the
; applications in /apps, Up/Down to choose, Enter to start the selected one in the
; background. CTRL+SHIFT+` opens the same box as a raw command line instead, for
; anything /apps does not list - a list of what is worth starting can never be
; complete, and a command with arguments has to stay possible. Pressing the chord
; you are already in closes the box; pressing the other one switches mode and keeps
; what you have typed, since a name that matched no application is exactly the thing
; you then want to run as a command.
;
; With no console page this is the only way to start a program without quitting
; first, exactly as in tile.cat. While it is open it takes every key, so nothing is
; typed into the window behind it, and the backquote is grabbed for the whole session
; so it works with nothing open too. The line editing, the application list and the
; spawn live in launch.cat, shared with tile.cat; the keys, the box and the rule
; about who gets the keyboard are policy and stay here.
;
; The bar carries a launcher button at its left-hand end, before the first tab, which
; opens the same box in application mode. A chord is not discoverable - CTRL+` is
; something you have to be told - and with nothing open the desktop otherwise offers
; the pointer nothing at all to click on, which is a machine that looks broken. The
; button is the visible half of what the chord does, so it goes through toggle_prompt
; too and the two can never disagree about whether the box is up.
;
; Clicking a tab also switches to it, and every tab carries a small "x" at its
; right-hand end that closes that window. There is no kernel call for closing one -
; a surface belongs to the program that made it and goes away when that program
; does - so the button does the only thing it can: it sends SIGTERM to the owning
; pid, which wlist reports, exactly as pkill would. A program that installs a
; SIGTERM handler therefore decides for itself what closing means, and one that does
; not simply dies and takes its window with it (proc teardown calls gfx_reap).
;
; F1 quits and hands the screen back to the text console; the pointer deliberately
; cannot, so a misplaced click never throws you out of the desktop - the worst a
; stray click can do is close one window.
#include "lib/sys.cat"

#define FBW, 512
#define FBH, 384
#define FB_BYTES, 786432             ; FBW * FBH * 4 (the framebuffer)
#define BAR_H, 16                    ; tab bar along the bottom
#define CONTENT_H, 368               ; FBH - BAR_H: the page area above the bar
#define CONTENT_BYTES, 753664        ; FBW * CONTENT_H * 4 (also the biggest surface)
#define ARENA_BYTES, 1540096         ; FB_BYTES + CONTENT_BYTES

#define MAXREC, 8                    ; must match the kernel's MAX_SURF
#define WREC_BYTES, 28               ; sid, w, h, reqw, reqh, seq, pid
#define WREC_W, 4                    ; offsets within one record
#define WREC_H, 8
#define WREC_SEQ, 20
#define WREC_PID, 24                 ; who to signal when its close button is hit

; ---- text, for the tab labels and the command prompt ----
#define GW, 8                        ; glyph width
#define GH, 16                       ; glyph height
#define FONT_GLYPHS, 129             ; glyphs the converted font provides

#define KEY_RIGHT, 262               ; raylib keycodes, claimed with keygrab
#define KEY_LEFT, 263
#define KEY_DOWN, 264                ; the launcher's selection; not grabbed by name,
#define KEY_UP, 265                  ; they arrive because the prompt takes every key
#define KEY_F1, 290                  ; quit back to the raw text console
#define KEY_GRAVE, 96                ; with CTRL: the launcher. With CTRL+SHIFT: the
                                     ; same box as a raw command line
#define KEY_ESCAPE, 256
#define KEY_ENTER, 257
#define KEY_BACKSPACE, 259

#define START_W, 20                  ; the launcher button, before the first tab
#define START_ICON_X, 5              ; the icon inside it: a 2x2 grid of squares,
#define START_ICON_Y, 3              ; (START_W - 10) / 2 and (BAR_H - 10) / 2 in
#define START_CELL, 4                ; from the button's corner. 4px squares, 2px apart
#define START_PITCH, 6               ; START_CELL + the gap: one square to the next

#define TAB_W, 56                    ; one tab button, including its 2px gutter
#define CLOSE_W, 14                  ; the close button's hit area, at the tab's
                                     ; right-hand end and inside its gutter
#define CLOSE_LEFT, 41               ; TAB_W - 1 - CLOSE_W: where that area starts
#define CLOSE_RIGHT, 55              ; TAB_W - 1: and where the gutter takes over
#define LABEL_X, 13                  ; the two-glyph label, centred in the 40px the
                                     ; close button leaves: 1 + (40 - 2*GW) / 2
#define CLOSE_X, 44                  ; the "x" glyph, centred in the button area:
                                     ; CLOSE_LEFT + (CLOSE_W - GW) / 2

#define BG_COLOUR, 0x001E2A38        ; dark slate page background
#define BAR_BG, 0x000E141B           ; behind the tabs
#define TAB_ON, 0x003A6EA5           ; the current page's tab
#define TAB_OFF, 0x00243544
#define TEXT_ON, 0x00FFFFFF
#define TEXT_OFF, 0x008FA6BC
#define CURSOR_COLOUR, 0x00FFFFFF
#define PROMPT_BG, 0x00101820        ; the command prompt: box, its edge when the
#define PROMPT_EDGE, 0x005A8FD0      ; last line ran, and its edge when it did not
#define PROMPT_BAD, 0x00C05040
#define PROMPT_TEXT, 0x00E8F0F5
#define PROMPT_H, 28                 ; box height: one glyph plus a margin
#define PROMPT_PAD, 6                ; (PROMPT_H - GH) / 2, the margin itself
#define PROMPT_INSET, 32             ; gap to the screen edge either side
#define PROMPT_Y, 140                ; the box, with the match list hanging below it
#define PROMPT_SEL, 0x002A4A66       ; the selected match's row
#define LIST_BG, 0x00161E28          ; behind the match list, a shade off the box so
                                     ; the two read as one panel rather than one thing
#define LIST_ROWS, 5                 ; matches on screen at once
#define LIST_ROW_H, 18               ; GH plus a little air
#define LIST_DESC_X, 104             ; PROMPT_PAD + 12 glyphs: the name column's width

main:
    sys_sbrk ARENA_BYTES             ; r0 = base of the new region, or -1
    cmp r0, -1
    je .fail
    mov [fbptr], r0                  ; framebuffer first
    add r0, FB_BYTES
    mov [surfptr], r0                ; surface scratch after it

    sys_gacquire [fbptr], 0          ; scan the display out of our own buffer. We
    cmp r0, -1                       ; draw no console, so its geometry is left
    je .fail                         ; alone (0) - nothing here reads it

    sys_keygrab KEY_LEFT, 1          ; claim the keys we page with. A grab matches on
    sys_keygrab KEY_RIGHT, 1         ; the keycode alone, so this covers the CTRL
                                     ; chord that actually pages, and it keeps an
                                     ; arrow off a console we are not showing
    sys_keygrab KEY_F1, 1            ; ... the one that gets you out
    sys_keygrab KEY_GRAVE, 1         ; ... and the one that opens the prompt
    sys_getpid                       ; so launch_reap knows whose children to bury
    mov [selfpid], r0
    call launch_apps_init            ; read /apps once: it changes when something is
                                     ; installed, not while we are compositing

    .loop:
        mov r1, [selfpid]
        call launch_reap             ; free the slot of anything we started that has
                                     ; since exited, before it fills the table
        call read_pages
        call fit_pages               ; nothing may be bigger than the page it is on
        call handle_keys             ; F1 is the only way out
        cmp r0, 1
        je .quit
        call handle_mouse
        call clamp_page
        call sync_grab               ; the keyboard follows the page
        call post_input              ; clicks must reach clients on skipped frames
        call need_redraw             ; r0 = 0 when last frame is still correct
        cmp r0, 0
        je .flush
        call clear_content
        call draw_page
        call draw_bar
        call draw_prompt             ; over the page and the bar, under the pointer
        call draw_cursor
        .flush:
        sys_gpresent                 ; always: the present blocks until the display
        sys_yield                    ; has taken the frame, which paces this loop
        jmp .loop

    .quit:
        sys_grelease
        sys_exit 0

    .fail:
        sys_exit 1


; ==========================
;   Page list and input
; ==========================

; Refresh the page list: one page per live surface, and nothing else - so with no
; windows open there are no pages and the desktop is a bare background.
read_pages:
    sys_wlist wlistbuf, MAXREC
    cmp r0, 0
    jige .ok
    mov r0, 0                        ; treat an error as "no surfaces"
    .ok:
    mov [pagecount], r0
    ret


; Give every window the whole page. A page *is* the screen minus the tab bar, and
; only one of them is visible at a time, so there is nothing for a window to share
; the space with and no reason to leave any of it empty: a 400x300 editor floating
; in the middle of a 512x368 page wastes a third of a screen that is already small,
; and every pixel it does not claim is background nobody can use. So the size a
; client asks for is treated as a first guess and the page area is what it gets -
; the same bargain tile.cat strikes with its cells, arrived at from the opposite
; direction. wcreate's request survives in the listing (WREC_REQW/H) for anything
; that wants to know what was wanted.
;
; The maximum this used to enforce comes along for free, and it was never optional:
; the kernel bounds a surface by the *display* and knows nothing about the tab bar,
; so a full-height window is not merely clipped here but invisible - surfptr is
; exactly one page big, so wread on a taller surface is refused and the page comes
; out blank. term.cat found that by asking for the whole screen.
;
; wresize is skipped when the size already matches, so the config counter only moves
; when something really changed and this can run unconditionally every frame. The
; listing was read before the resize, so it is corrected in place for the rest of
; this frame; the client hears about it through wconfig and reshapes itself.
fit_pages:
    push r4                          ; page index
    push r5                          ; its record
    push r6                          ; the width every page gets
    push r7                          ; and the height
    mov r6, FBW
    mov r7, CONTENT_H
    mov r4, 0
    .each:
        mov r0, [pagecount]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, r4
        umul r5, WREC_BYTES
        add r5, wlistbuf
        mov r0, r5                   ; already the right size? then leave it alone,
        add r0, WREC_W               ; so its config counter stays still
        mov r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, r6
        jne .set
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, WREC_H
        mov r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, r7
        je .next
        .set:
        mov r0, r6                   ; packed (w << 16) | h
        shl r0, 16
        or r0, r7
        mov r1, [r5]                 ; sid
        sys_wresize r1, r0
        cmp r0, -1
        je .next                     ; refused: draw what is there, clipped
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, WREC_W
        mov [r0], r6
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, WREC_H
        mov [r0], r7
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .each
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Drain the key events the ISR diverted to us. CTRL+Left / CTRL+Right step one page
; and stop at the ends rather than wrapping; an unmodified F1 asks to quit.
; Everything else - the bare arrows included, and every release - belongs to the
; window on the current page, if there is one, and goes over untouched.
; -> r0 = 1 if F1 was pressed.
handle_keys:
    push r4                          ; the whole event, which is what a window wants
    push r5                          ; its keycode, which is what we match on
    push r6                          ; quit requested
    mov r6, 0
    .drain:
        sys_gkey
        cmp r0, -1
        je .done                     ; ring empty
        mov r4, r0
        mov r5, r4
        kev_code r5

        cmp r5, KEY_GRAVE            ; CTRL+` in and out of the launcher, on the
        jne .notgrave                ; press edge, whichever page is up
        mov r0, r4
        and r0, KEV_UP
        cmp r0, 0
        jne .drain
        mov r0, r4
        kev_mods r0
        mov r1, r0
        and r0, KMOD_CTRL
        cmp r0, 0
        je .notgrave                 ; a bare backquote is just a character
        and r1, KMOD_SHIFT           ; SHIFT is what asks for the raw command line
        cmp r1, 0
        je .wantapps
        mov r1, LMODE_RAW
        jmp .gotmode
        .wantapps:
        mov r1, LMODE_APPS
        .gotmode:
        call toggle_prompt
        jmp .drain
        .notgrave:
        mov r0, [promptup]           ; while it is up it is the only thing typed at,
        cmp r0, 0                    ; paging and F1 included - ESCAPE is the way out
        jne .toprompt

        mov r0, r4                   ; a release is nobody's shortcut
        and r0, KEV_UP
        cmp r0, 0
        jne .toclient
        mov r0, r4                   ; which chord it is decides whose key it is
        kev_mods r0
        cmp r0, KMOD_CTRL            ; CTRL+Left / CTRL+Right page; any other CTRL
        jne .unmod                   ; chord is the window's
        cmp r5, KEY_LEFT
        je .left
        cmp r5, KEY_RIGHT
        je .right
        jmp .toclient
        .unmod:
        cmp r0, 0                    ; a bare arrow is a cursor movement and SHIFT+Left
        jne .toclient                ; a selection: both belong to the window
        cmp r5, KEY_F1
        je .wantquit
        .toclient:
        call page_sid                ; not ours: it belongs to the window on show
        cmp r0, -1
        je .drain                    ; page 0, or nothing there - drop it
        sys_wkeypost r0, r4
        jmp .drain
        .wantquit:
        mov r6, 1
        jmp .drain
        .left:
        mov r0, [page]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .drain
        sub r0, 1
        mov [page], r0
        jmp .drain
        .right:
        mov r0, [page]
        add r0, 1
        mov r1, [pagecount]
        cmp r0, r1
        juge .drain
        mov [page], r0
        jmp .drain

        .toprompt:                   ; the prompt's own line editing
        mov r0, r4
        and r0, KEV_UP
        cmp r0, 0
        jne .drain                   ; releases are nothing to type
        cmp r5, KEY_ENTER
        je .runit
        cmp r5, KEY_ESCAPE
        je .cancel
        cmp r5, KEY_BACKSPACE
        je .rubout
        cmp r5, KEY_UP
        je .selup
        cmp r5, KEY_DOWN
        je .seldown
        mov r1, r4                   ; anything else: the character it makes, which
        kev_char r1                  ; is 0 for the keys that make none
        call launch_putc
        jmp .edited
        .rubout:
        call launch_erase
        .edited:
        mov r0, 0                    ; editing clears a failure from the last run
        mov [promptbad], r0
        jmp .drain
        .selup:
        mov r1, -1                   ; moving the selection is not editing, so a
        call launch_move             ; failed run stays reported
        jmp .drain
        .seldown:
        mov r1, 1
        call launch_move
        jmp .drain
        .runit:
        call launch_commit
        cmp r0, -1
        je .badcmd
        call close_prompt            ; started: get out of the way, and it turns up
        jmp .drain                   ; as a new page a frame or two from now
        .badcmd:
        mov r0, 1                    ; no such command - stay up, in red, so the
        mov [promptbad], r0          ; typo can be fixed rather than retyped
        jmp .drain
        .cancel:
        call close_prompt
        jmp .drain
    .done:
    mov r0, r6
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Open, close, or switch the mode of the prompt box. r1 = the LMODE_* the chord just
; asked for: the same one we are already in closes the box, the other one switches to
; it and keeps the line.
;
; Opening takes the keyboard immediately rather than waiting for the next sync_grab:
; with nothing open we are not holding it, the console is still reading, and the
; first thing typed at a prompt that has just opened would otherwise land in the
; shell behind us.
toggle_prompt:
    mov r0, [promptup]
    cmp r0, 0
    je open_prompt
    mov r0, [launch_mode]
    cmp r0, r1
    je close_prompt
    call launch_setmode              ; the other chord: change what the line means
    mov r0, 0
    mov [promptbad], r0
    ret
open_prompt:
    call launch_open                 ; r1 = the mode it opens in
    mov r0, 0
    mov [promptbad], r0
    mov r0, 1
    mov [promptup], r0
    call sync_grab
    ret

close_prompt:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [promptup], r0
    mov [promptbad], r0
    call sync_grab                   ; and the keyboard goes back to the page
    ret


; Poll the pointer and act on a left-click in the tab bar, on the press edge only
; so holding the button does not retrigger every frame. The pointer cannot quit -
; F1 is the only way out, so a stray click can never drop you off the desktop.
handle_mouse:
    push r4                          ; this frame's button mask
    sys_mouse mousebuf
    mov r0, mousebuf
    add r0, 8
    mov r4, [r0]
    mov r0, r4                       ; bit 0 = left button
    and r0, 1
    cmp r0, 0
    je .release                      ; not pressed
    mov r1, [prevbtn]
    and r1, 1
    cmp r1, 0
    jne .store                       ; still held from last frame -> not an edge
    mov r1, 1                        ; a press begins here; post_input works out
    mov [presstart], r1              ; which window, if any, it belongs to
    call tab_hit
    .store:
    mov [prevbtn], r4
    pop r4
    ret
    .release:
    mov r1, 0                        ; the button is up, so the grab ends with it
    mov [grabbed], r1
    jmp .store


; If the pointer is over the tab bar, act on whatever is under it: the button at the
; left opens the launcher, the "x" at a tab's right-hand end closes that window, and
; anywhere else on a tab switches to it.
;
; The launcher button is a toggle, exactly as its chord is - clicking it while the box
; is already up in application mode closes it, and clicking it while the box is up as a
; raw command line switches modes and keeps the line, since going through
; toggle_prompt means the pointer and the keyboard cannot disagree about what the box
; is doing.
tab_hit:
    mov r0, mousebuf
    add r0, 4
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; y
    cmp r0, CONTENT_H
    jul .done                        ; above the bar -> not a bar click
    mov r0, mousebuf
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; x
    cmp r0, START_W
    jul .start
    sub r0, START_W                  ; the tabs begin past the launcher button
    mov r1, TAB_W
    udiv r0, r1                      ; r0 = tab index, r1 = x within that tab
    mov r2, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, r2
    juge .done                       ; past the last tab
    cmp r1, CLOSE_LEFT
    jul .select                      ; left of the button
    cmp r1, CLOSE_RIGHT
    juge .select                     ; in the gutter, past the button
    call close_page
    ret
    .select:
    mov [page], r0
    .done:
    ret
    .start:
    mov r1, LMODE_APPS               ; the pointer only ever asks for applications; a
    jmp toggle_prompt                ; raw command line is a thing you type, so it
                                     ; keeps its chord and no button of its own


; Close the window on page r0 by asking its program to exit. SIGTERM rather than
; SIGKILL: closing a window is a request, and a program that wants to save first,
; or refuse, is entitled to. The window itself disappears when the process does,
; so nothing here touches the page list - the next read_pages simply finds one
; fewer surface and clamp_page moves off it.
close_page:
    push r4                          ; the record for that page
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r4, r0
    juge .done                       ; the page list moved under us
    umul r4, WREC_BYTES
    add r4, wlistbuf
    add r4, WREC_PID
    mov r0, [r4]
    sys_kill r0, SIGTERM
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


; ==========================
;   Change detection
; ==========================
; Composing a frame costs a 736KB clear, a full glyph pass or a 300KB window fetch
; and blit, and the tab bar - and the overwhelming majority of frames are identical
; to the one before. So each source of change carries a counter or a value we can
; compare in a few instructions, and if none of them moved we present the frame we
; already have and do none of that work.
;
; The content check runs unconditionally rather than short-circuiting, because a
; window can commit new pixels in the same frame that the pointer moves, and
; noticing only the pointer would leave the window a frame stale.

; -> r0 = 1 if anything on screen has changed since the last frame.
need_redraw:
    push r4                          ; accumulated "something changed"
    mov r4, 0
    mov r0, 0
    mov [needread], r0

    call check_surface               ; whatever is in the content area
    or r4, r0

    mov r0, [page]                   ; which page, and how many tabs
    mov r1, [lastpage]
    mov [lastpage], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    je .c1
    mov r4, 1
    .c1:
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    mov r1, [lastcount]
    mov [lastcount], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    je .c2
    mov r4, 1
    .c2:
    mov r0, mousebuf                 ; the cursor is drawn into the frame, so any
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; pointer movement means a redraw
    mov r1, [lastmx]
    mov [lastmx], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    je .c3
    mov r4, 1
    .c3:
    mov r0, mousebuf
    add r0, 4
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov r1, [lastmy]
    mov [lastmy], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    je .c4
    mov r4, 1
    .c4:
    mov r0, [promptup]               ; the prompt box and everything drawn in it -
    shl r0, 24                       ; what has been typed, which mode it is in,
    mov r1, [launch_mode]            ; which match is selected and how many there
    shl r1, 23                       ; are - in one word, since all of it is drawn
    or r0, r1                        ; into the frame like everything else
    mov r1, [launch_len]
    shl r1, 16
    or r0, r1
    mov r1, [launch_sel]
    shl r1, 8
    or r0, r1
    mov r1, [launch_nmatch]
    shl r1, 4
    or r0, r1
    mov r1, [promptbad]
    or r0, r1
    mov r1, [lastprompt]
    mov [lastprompt], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    je .c5
    mov r4, 1
    .c5:
    mov r0, r4
    pop r4
    ret


; Report whether the current page's window has committed new pixels, and if so set
; needread so draw_surface pulls them across. A different surface in this slot also
; counts as new - the id is a slot index and gets recycled.
; Uses only r0-r3 so need_redraw's accumulator survives.
check_surface:
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .nothing                      ; nothing open: the content area is empty
    mov r0, [page]
    umul r0, WREC_BYTES
    add r0, wlistbuf
    mov r2, r0                       ; this page's wlist record
    mov r0, [r2]                     ; sid
    mov r1, [lastsid]
    mov [lastsid], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    jne .fetch
    mov r0, r2
    add r0, WREC_SEQ
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; commit counter
    mov r1, [lastseq]
    mov [lastseq], r0
    cmp r0, r1
    jne .fetch
    mov r0, 0
    ret
    .fetch:
    mov r0, r2                       ; keep the counter in step on this path too
    add r0, WREC_SEQ
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov [lastseq], r0
    mov r0, 1
    mov [needread], r0
    ret
    .nothing:
    mov r0, -1                       ; no surface at all, which is a change from
    mov [lastsid], r0                ; whatever was here before
    mov r0, 0
    ret


; Route the pointer to the window on the current page, in its own coordinates, and
; tell every other window it does not have it. Runs every frame, before the redraw
; decision - a click has to be delivered even on a frame we would otherwise skip,
; and the program acting on it will bump its commit counter and cause the redraw.
;
; A press belongs to the window it started on, and to no other, for as long as it
; is held. That rule has to live here because we are the only thing that knows
; where the pointer was when the button went down: a client sees only a level, and
; turns it into a click by noticing it was 0 last frame and is 1 now. So a window
; that was not being pointed at - it was a background page, and every background
; page is told (-1, -1, 0) - has a remembered 0, and the first frame it becomes
; visible under a button that is still down looks exactly like a fresh press on it.
; That is how closing one window used to close the one behind it when their buttons
; happened to line up. Withholding the button until the next press keeps every
; client honest without any of them needing to know about it, and it is the same
; reason dragging out of one window and into another does not click the second.
;
; The owner is claimed below rather than at the press edge so that it is decided by
; the very test that gates the buttons, and the two cannot drift apart.
post_input:
    push r4                          ; index
    push r5                          ; record
    push r6                          ; this page's record, or 0
    mov r6, 0
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .scan                         ; nothing open: everyone gets "not you"
    mov r0, [page]
    umul r0, WREC_BYTES
    add r0, wlistbuf
    mov r6, r0
    .scan:
    mov r4, 0
    .each:
        mov r0, [pagecount]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, r4
        umul r5, WREC_BYTES
        add r5, wlistbuf
        cmp r5, r6
        je .visible
        mov r0, -1                   ; not the page on screen: no pointer
        mov [postbuf], r0
        mov r1, postbuf
        add r1, 4
        mov [r1], r0
        add r1, 4
        mov [r1], 0
        jmp .send
        .visible:
        call surface_rect            ; where we drew it: sx, sy
        mov r0, mousebuf             ; local x = pointer x - sx
        mov r0, [r0]
        mov r1, [sx]
        sub r0, r1
        mov [postbuf], r0
        mov r1, mousebuf             ; local y = pointer y - sy
        add r1, 4
        mov r1, [r1]
        mov r2, [sy]
        sub r1, r2
        mov r2, postbuf
        add r2, 4
        mov [r2], r1
        mov r2, postbuf              ; buttons only while actually inside it
        add r2, 8
        mov [r2], 0
        cmp r0, 0
        jil .send
        cmp r1, 0
        jil .send
        mov r3, r5
        add r3, WREC_W
        mov r3, [r3]
        cmp r0, r3
        juge .send
        mov r3, r5
        add r3, WREC_H
        mov r3, [r3]
        cmp r1, r3
        juge .send
        mov r3, [presstart]          ; the pointer really is on this window, so a
        cmp r3, 0                    ; press starting this frame is this window's
        je .owner
        mov r3, [r5]
        mov [grabsid], r3
        mov r3, 1
        mov [grabbed], r3
        mov r3, 0
        mov [presstart], r3
        .owner:
        mov r3, [grabbed]            ; a button that went down somewhere else stays
        cmp r3, 0                    ; somewhere else's for as long as it is held
        je .send
        mov r3, [grabsid]
        mov r1, [r5]
        cmp r3, r1
        jne .send
        mov r1, mousebuf
        add r1, 8
        mov r1, [r1]
        mov [r2], r1
        .send:
        mov r0, [r5]                 ; sid
        sys_wpost r0, postbuf
        add r4, 1
        jmp .each
    .done:
    mov r0, 0                        ; a press that began over no window at all - the
    mov [presstart], r0              ; tab bar, or the bare desktop - belongs to none
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Where the current page's window is drawn: centred in the content area. Writes
; sx, sy. Uses the record at r6.
;
; fit_pages has normally made that the whole content area, so both differences are
; zero and this is simply the origin. The centring is what happens when they are
; not: a window whose resize was refused - the surface went away between the
; listing and the call - sits in the middle of its page rather than in a corner.
; One that left it *bigger* than the page is pinned to the top-left corner and
; clipped rather than centred, because the arithmetic is unsigned: half of "minus
; eight" is two billion, and everything drawn at it lands off the screen.
surface_rect:
    mov r0, r6
    add r0, WREC_W
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov r1, FBW
    sub r1, r0
    cmp r1, 0
    jig .cx
    mov r1, 0
    .cx:
    mov r2, 2
    udiv r1, r2
    mov [sx], r1
    mov r0, r6
    add r0, WREC_H
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov r1, CONTENT_H
    sub r1, r0
    cmp r1, 0
    jig .cy
    mov r1, 0
    .cy:
    mov r2, 2
    udiv r1, r2
    mov [sy], r1
    ret


; The surface shown on the current page -> r0 = its sid, or -1 if there is nothing
; open or the page list has moved under us.
page_sid:
    mov r0, [page]
    mov r1, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .none
    umul r0, WREC_BYTES
    add r0, wlistbuf
    mov r0, [r0]
    ret
    .none:
    mov r0, -1
    ret


; Hold the keyboard only while there is a window to give it to. With nothing open
; the grab is dropped so the console gets its keys back and the shell behind us
; works exactly as it does without a window manager running; with a page up we take
; the lot and relay it to that window. Left/Right/F1 are grabbed by name throughout,
; so CTRL+Left and CTRL+Right always page.
sync_grab:
    push r4                          ; the grab we want
    mov r4, 0
    mov r0, [promptup]               ; the prompt outranks the pages: while it is up
    cmp r0, 0                        ; the keys are ours even with nothing open
    jne .want
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .have
    .want:
    mov r4, 1
    .have:
    mov r0, [lastgrab]
    cmp r0, r4
    je .done                         ; already in that state
    mov [lastgrab], r4
    sys_keygrab -1, r4
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


; Keep the selection inside the live page list - the page we were on disappears
; when the program owning it exits, and the list empties altogether when the last
; window goes.
clamp_page:
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .empty
    mov r1, [page]
    cmp r1, r0
    jul .done
    sub r0, 1
    mov [page], r0
    ret
    .empty:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [page], r0
    .done:
    ret


; ==========================
;   Drawing
; ==========================

; Draw whichever page is current into the content area. With nothing open there is
; nothing to draw: clear_content has already left the bare background.
draw_page:
    mov r0, [pagecount]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    jmp draw_surface
    .done:
    ret


; A surface page: blit the client's pixels centred in the content area. The fetch
; out of the kernel (wread - a copy of the whole window) only happens when the
; client has actually committed a new frame; otherwise the scratch buffer still
; holds its pixels and we redraw straight from that.
draw_surface:
    push r4                          ; this page's wlist record
    push r6                          ; surface_rect's input
    mov r0, [page]
    umul r0, WREC_BYTES
    add r0, wlistbuf
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [needread]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .havepixels
    mov r0, [r4]                     ; sid
    sys_wread r0, [surfptr]
    cmp r0, -1
    je .done
    .havepixels:
    mov r0, r4                       ; the size it currently has
    add r0, WREC_W
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov [bw], r0
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, WREC_H
    mov r0, [r0]
    mov [bh], r0
    mov r6, r4                       ; centred, the same way post_input assumes
    call surface_rect
    mov r0, [sx]
    mov [bx], r0
    mov r0, [sy]
    mov [by], r0
    mov r0, [surfptr]
    mov [bsrc], r0
    call blit_rect
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r4
    ret


; The tab bar: the launcher button at the left, then one button per page, the current
; one highlighted, labelled "w<n>", with an "x" on each to close it. With nothing open
; it is the button and an otherwise empty strip.
draw_bar:
    push r4                          ; page index
    push r5                          ; tab left edge
    mov [fx], 0                      ; the strip behind the buttons
    mov [fy], CONTENT_H
    mov [fw], FBW
    mov [fh], BAR_H
    mov [fc], BAR_BG
    call fill_rect
    call draw_start
    mov r4, 0
    .tabs:
        mov r0, [pagecount]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, r4
        umul r5, TAB_W
        add r5, START_W              ; the tabs start past the launcher button
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, TAB_W
        cmp r0, FBW
        jug .done                    ; no room left for another button
        mov r0, r5                   ; the button, with a 1px gutter each side
        add r0, 1
        mov [fx], r0
        mov [fy], CONTENT_H
        mov [fw], TAB_W - 2
        mov [fh], BAR_H
        mov r0, [page]
        cmp r0, r4
        je .on
        mov [fc], TAB_OFF
        jmp .paint
        .on:
        mov [fc], TAB_ON
        .paint:
        call fill_rect
        mov r1, r5                   ; then its label and close button
        mov r2, r4
        call draw_tab_label
        add r4, 1
        jmp .tabs
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The launcher button, at the left-hand end of the bar. It is drawn like a tab and lit
; like the current one while the box is open, because that is what it is: a control
; that shows the state it toggles.
;
; The icon is a 2x2 grid of squares rather than a letter. The font here is the console's
; 8x16 blob, which has glyphs and nothing else, so anything pictorial has to be drawn -
; and four rectangles is a picture the fill_rect already on hand can make. It reads as
; "the things you can run", which is exactly what the button opens.
draw_start:
    push r4                          ; icon row
    push r5                          ; icon column
    mov [fx], 0
    mov [fy], CONTENT_H
    mov [fw], START_W - 1            ; a 1px gutter, as the tabs have either side
    mov [fh], BAR_H
    mov r0, [promptup]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .off
    mov [fc], TAB_ON
    jmp .paint
    .off:
    mov [fc], TAB_OFF
    .paint:
    call fill_rect
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        cmp r4, 2
        juge .done
        mov r5, 0
        .cols:
            cmp r5, 2
            juge .nextrow
            mov r0, r5
            umul r0, START_PITCH
            add r0, START_ICON_X
            mov [fx], r0
            mov r0, r4
            umul r0, START_PITCH
            add r0, CONTENT_H + START_ICON_Y
            mov [fy], r0
            mov [fw], START_CELL
            mov [fh], START_CELL
            mov [fc], TEXT_ON
            call fill_rect
            add r5, 1
            jmp .cols
        .nextrow:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Draw what is inside one tab: a two-glyph label "w<n>", numbered from 1 so the
; first window is "w1", and the "x" that closes it. r1 = the tab's left edge,
; r2 = page index.
draw_tab_label:
    push r4                          ; the tab's left edge
    push r5                          ; page index
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    mov [gy], CONTENT_H
    mov r0, [page]
    cmp r0, r5
    je .on
    mov [gc], TEXT_OFF
    jmp .draw
    .on:
    mov [gc], TEXT_ON
    .draw:
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, LABEL_X
    mov [gx], r0
    mov [gch], 119                   ; 'w'
    call draw_glyph
    mov r0, r5                       ; the page number, single digit, 1-based
    add r0, 49
    mov [gch], r0
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, LABEL_X + GW
    mov [gx], r0
    call draw_glyph
    mov [gch], 120                   ; 'x': tab_hit closes the window on a click here
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, CLOSE_X
    mov [gx], r0
    call draw_glyph
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Draw the mouse cursor: a small filled block at the pointer position.
draw_cursor:
    mov r0, mousebuf
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; x
    mov [fx], r0
    mov r0, mousebuf
    add r0, 4
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; y
    mov [fy], r0
    mov [fw], 6
    mov [fh], 9
    mov [fc], CURSOR_COLOUR
    call fill_rect
    ret


; Clear the page area (everything above the tab bar) to BG_COLOUR using the
; doubling copy trick: seed one pixel, then repeatedly copy the filled span onto
; itself so it fills in ~log2(N) fast `cpy` blocks.
clear_content:
    push r4
    push r5
    mov r0, [fbptr]
    mov [r0], BG_COLOUR              ; seed pixel
    mov r4, 4
    .fill:
        cmp r4, CONTENT_BYTES
        juge .done
        mov r5, CONTENT_BYTES
        sub r5, r4                   ; remaining
        cmp r4, r5
        jule .use
        mov r0, [fbptr]
        add r0, r4
        mov r1, [fbptr]
        cpy r1, r5
        jmp .done
        .use:
        mov r0, [fbptr]
        add r0, r4
        mov r1, [fbptr]
        cpy r1, r4
        add r4, r4
        jmp .fill
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ==========================
;   Primitives
; ==========================

; The command prompt: a box across the middle of the content area holding what has
; been typed so far. Drawn after the page and the bar so it sits over both - it is a
; mode this manager is in rather than another page, and while it is up nothing
; underneath is being typed at anyway. A run that found no such program in /bin
; turns the edge red and leaves the line alone, so a mistyped name can be corrected
; instead of retyped.
draw_prompt:
    push r4                          ; pen x
    push r5                          ; cursor into the line
    mov r0, [promptup]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done

    mov [fx], PROMPT_INSET - 1       ; the edge, as a rectangle one pixel larger all
    mov [fy], PROMPT_Y - 1           ; round with the box painted inside it
    mov [fw], FBW - PROMPT_INSET - PROMPT_INSET + 2
    mov [fh], PROMPT_H + 2
    mov r0, [promptbad]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .edgeok
    mov [fc], PROMPT_BAD
    jmp .edge
    .edgeok:
    mov [fc], PROMPT_EDGE
    .edge:
    call fill_rect

    mov [fx], PROMPT_INSET
    mov [fy], PROMPT_Y
    mov [fw], FBW - PROMPT_INSET - PROMPT_INSET
    mov [fh], PROMPT_H
    mov [fc], PROMPT_BG
    call fill_rect

    mov [gy], PROMPT_Y + PROMPT_PAD  ; one glyph row, centred in the box
    mov [gc], PROMPT_TEXT
    mov r4, PROMPT_INSET + PROMPT_PAD
    mov r0, 62                       ; '>' for a command line, ':' for a search, so
    mov r1, [launch_mode]            ; which mode the box is in is legible without
    cmp r1, LMODE_RAW                ; having to remember which chord was pressed
    je .prefix
    mov r0, 58
    .prefix:
    mov [gch], r0                    ; the prefix, a space, then the line
    mov [gx], r4
    call draw_glyph
    add r4, GW
    add r4, GW
    mov r5, launch_line
    .each:
        mov8 r0, [r5]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .caret
        mov [gch], r0
        mov [gx], r4
        call draw_glyph
        add r4, GW
        add r5, 1
        jmp .each
    .caret:
    mov [fx], r4                     ; a solid block where the next one would go
    mov [fy], PROMPT_Y + PROMPT_PAD
    mov [fw], GW
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], PROMPT_TEXT
    call fill_rect
    call draw_matches
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The match list, under the box: a panel exactly as many rows tall as there are
; matches to show, one row per application the line matched, the selected one on a
; highlight bar, name then description. Nothing at all in raw mode, where there are no
; matches by definition, and nothing when a search found none - an empty list under an
; empty box says "no such application" plainly enough.
;
; The panel is drawn first, edged like the box and butted straight up against it, so
; the two are one surface with a divider rather than text floating over whatever page
; happens to be underneath. Sizing it to the rows in hand rather than to LIST_ROWS is
; the same idea: an empty strip below the last match would be a list claiming to have
; more in it.
draw_matches:
    push r4                          ; row on screen
    push r5                          ; the match it shows
    push r6                          ; that row's top edge; first, the rows on screen
    push r7                          ; the record's name / desc
    mov r1, LIST_ROWS
    call launch_first
    mov r5, r0
    mov r0, [launch_nmatch]
    sub r0, r5
    cmp r0, 0
    jile .done                       ; nothing matched: no panel either
    cmp r0, LIST_ROWS
    jule .havecount
    mov r0, LIST_ROWS
    .havecount:
    mov r6, r0
    mov [fx], PROMPT_INSET - 1       ; the edge, meeting the box's own
    mov [fy], PROMPT_Y + PROMPT_H + 1
    mov [fw], FBW - PROMPT_INSET - PROMPT_INSET + 2
    mov r0, r6
    umul r0, LIST_ROW_H
    add r0, 2
    mov [fh], r0
    mov [fc], PROMPT_EDGE
    call fill_rect
    mov [fx], PROMPT_INSET           ; and the panel inside it
    mov [fy], PROMPT_Y + PROMPT_H + 2
    mov [fw], FBW - PROMPT_INSET - PROMPT_INSET
    mov r0, r6
    umul r0, LIST_ROW_H
    mov [fh], r0
    mov [fc], LIST_BG
    call fill_rect
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        cmp r4, LIST_ROWS
        juge .done
        mov r0, [launch_nmatch]
        cmp r5, r0
        juge .done                   ; ran out of matches before running out of room
        mov r6, r4
        umul r6, LIST_ROW_H
        add r6, PROMPT_Y + PROMPT_H + 2

        mov r0, [launch_sel]         ; the selected row gets a bar behind it
        cmp r0, r5
        jne .text
        mov [fx], PROMPT_INSET
        mov [fy], r6
        mov [fw], FBW - PROMPT_INSET - PROMPT_INSET
        mov [fh], LIST_ROW_H
        mov [fc], PROMPT_SEL
        call fill_rect

        .text:
        mov r0, r6
        add r0, 1
        mov [gy], r0
        mov [gc], PROMPT_TEXT
        mov r1, r5
        call launch_match_name
        mov r7, r0
        mov r1, r7
        mov r2, PROMPT_INSET + PROMPT_PAD
        mov r3, 12
        call draw_clipped
        mov [gc], TEXT_OFF           ; the description, dimmer, in its own column
        mov r1, r5
        call launch_match_desc
        mov r7, r0
        cmp r7, 0
        je .next
        mov r1, r7
        mov r2, PROMPT_INSET + LIST_DESC_X
        mov r3, 44
        call draw_clipped
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        add r5, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Draw at most r3 glyphs of the string at r1, starting at x = r2, on the row and in
; the colour already set in gy/gc.
draw_clipped:
    push r4                          ; read cursor
    push r5                          ; pen x
    push r6                          ; glyphs left
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    mov r6, r3
    .each:
        cmp r6, 0
        jile .done
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .done
        mov [gch], r0
        mov [gx], r5
        call draw_glyph
        add r5, GW
        add r4, 1
        sub r6, 1
        jmp .each
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Draw one 8x16 glyph from the embedded console font. Inputs (globals): gx, gy
; (top-left pixel), gch (character), gc (colour). Rows whose font byte is zero
; are skipped - most of a glyph is empty.
draw_glyph:
    push r4                          ; glyph row
    push r5                          ; the row's font byte
    push r6                          ; destination address of the row
    push r7                          ; bit index within the row
    mov r0, [gch]
    cmp r0, FONT_GLYPHS
    juge .done                       ; unmapped code -> draw nothing
    umul r0, GH
    add r0, font                     ; font + ch*16
    mov [gsrc], r0
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        cmp r4, GH
        juge .done
        mov r0, [gy]
        add r0, r4                   ; py
        cmp r0, FBH
        juge .done                   ; off the bottom of the screen
        mov r5, [gsrc]
        add r5, r4
        mov8 r5, [r5]                ; 1bpp row, MSB = leftmost pixel
        cmp r5, 0
        je .nextrow                  ; blank row
        umul r0, FBW                 ; dst = fbptr + (py*FBW + gx)*4
        mov r1, [gx]
        add r0, r1
        umul r0, 4
        mov r6, [fbptr]
        add r6, r0
        mov r7, 0
        .bits:
            cmp r7, GW
            juge .nextrow
            mov r0, 7                ; leftmost pixel is bit 7
            sub r0, r7
            mov r1, r5
            shr r1, r0
            and r1, 1
            cmp r1, 0
            je .nextbit
            mov r0, [gx]
            add r0, r7
            cmp r0, FBW
            juge .nextrow            ; ran off the right edge
            mov r0, r7
            umul r0, 4
            add r0, r6
            mov r1, [gc]
            mov [r0], r1
            .nextbit:
            add r7, 1
            jmp .bits
        .nextrow:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Fill a rectangle with a solid colour, clipped to the screen. Inputs (globals):
; fx, fy, fw, fh, fc. Assumes x, y >= 0.
fill_rect:
    push r4                          ; row
    push r5                          ; py
    push r6                          ; clipped width
    push r7                          ; column counter
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        mov r0, [fh]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, [fy]
        add r5, r4                   ; py
        cmp r5, FBH
        juge .done
        mov r6, [fw]
        mov r0, [fx]
        add r0, r6
        cmp r0, FBW
        jule .wok
        mov r6, FBW
        mov r0, [fx]
        sub r6, r0
        .wok:
        cmp r6, 0
        jile .next
        mov r0, r5                   ; dst = fbptr + (py*FBW + x)*4
        umul r0, FBW
        mov r1, [fx]
        add r0, r1
        umul r0, 4
        mov r2, [fbptr]
        add r0, r2
        mov r1, [fc]
        mov r7, r6
        .cols:
            mov [r0], r1
            add r0, 4
            sub r7, 1
            cmp r7, 0
            jne .cols
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Blit a w x h image into the content area, clipped so it can never overwrite the
; tab bar. Inputs (globals): bsrc, bx, by, bw, bh. Assumes x, y >= 0.
blit_rect:
    push r4                          ; row
    push r5                          ; py
    push r6                          ; clipped width
    push r7                          ; src base
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        mov r0, [bh]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, [by]
        add r5, r4                   ; py
        cmp r5, CONTENT_H
        juge .done
        mov r6, [bw]
        mov r0, [bx]
        add r0, r6
        cmp r0, FBW
        jule .wok
        mov r6, FBW
        mov r0, [bx]
        sub r6, r0
        .wok:
        cmp r6, 0
        jile .next
        mov r0, r5                   ; dst = fbptr + (py*FBW + x)*4
        umul r0, FBW
        mov r1, [bx]
        add r0, r1
        umul r0, 4
        mov r2, [fbptr]
        add r0, r2                   ; R0 = dst
        mov r1, r4                   ; src = bsrc + (row * bw)*4
        mov r2, [bw]
        umul r1, r2
        umul r1, 4
        mov r7, [bsrc]
        add r1, r7                   ; R1 = src
        mov r2, r6                   ; length = clipped width * 4
        umul r2, 4
        cpy r1, r2
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ---- the console font -------------------------------------------------------
; The same 8x16 1bpp blob the kernel's PPU driver embeds, so the tab labels and the
; command prompt are set in the machine's own typeface. Path is relative to this
; source file (src/user/).
font:
    dfile "../../build/font.bin"


; ---- state ------------------------------------------------------------------
fbptr:
    d32 0                            ; base of our framebuffer (in our own memory)
surfptr:
    d32 0                            ; base of the surface scratch buffer
page:
    d32 0                            ; the page on screen
pagecount:
    d32 0                            ; live surfaces, and nothing else
lastgrab:
    d32 0                            ; 1 while we are holding the whole keyboard
selfpid:
    d32 0                            ; our own pid, for launch_reap
promptup:
    d32 0                            ; 1 while the command prompt is open
promptbad:
    d32 0                            ; 1 when the last line named nothing runnable
prevbtn:
    d32 0                            ; last frame's buttons, for press-edge detection
presstart:
    d32 0                            ; a press began this frame, owner not yet known
grabbed:
    d32 0                            ; a press is in progress and a window owns it
grabsid:
    d32 0                            ; which one - only meaningful while grabbed
; ---- what the frame on screen was composed from (see need_redraw) ----
lastpage:
    d32 -1                           ; -1 so the first frame always draws
lastcount:
    d32 0
lastmx:
    d32 -1
lastmy:
    d32 -1
lastprompt:
    d32 -1                           ; the prompt state the frame on screen was drawn
                                     ; from, packed by need_redraw
lastsid:
    d32 -1                           ; slot ids are recycled, so track it explicitly
lastseq:
    d32 0
needread:
    d32 0                            ; this frame needs a wread (new client pixels)
sx:
    d32 0                            ; where the current page's window is drawn
sy:
    d32 0
postbuf:
    res8 12                          ; wpost: x, y, buttons
mousebuf:
    res8 12                          ; x, y, buttons
wlistbuf:
    res8 MAXREC * WREC_BYTES
; fill_rect inputs
fx:
    d32 0
fy:
    d32 0
fw:
    d32 0
fh:
    d32 0
fc:
    d32 0
; blit_rect inputs
bsrc:
    d32 0
bx:
    d32 0
by:
    d32 0
bw:
    d32 0
bh:
    d32 0
; draw_glyph inputs
gx:
    d32 0
gy:
    d32 0
gch:
    d32 0
gc:
    d32 0
gsrc:
    d32 0                            ; font address of the glyph being drawn

#include "lib/launch.cat"
